  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Left middle ear barotrauma (T70.0XXA)</content>. Middle ear barotrauma from descending with a blocked eustachian tube during a cold, on a background of chronic nasal blockage. The drum is intact and there is no vertigo, ringing or sensorineural loss to suggest inner-ear barotrauma or a perilymph fistula. Most cases settle in one to two weeks as the eustachian tube opens. [S]<list><item>Acetaminophen when needed; acceptable for pilots.</item><item>Gentle ear clearing (swallowing, yawning, gentle Valsalva) once the pain settles; no forceful blowing.</item><item>No flying, as pilot or passenger, until the ear clears, hearing is back to normal and the cold has gone.</item><item>Urgent return for spinning, worsening hearing, loud ringing, or ear discharge.</item><item>Ear check at the ten-week sinus review, or sooner if not clearing.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps (J32.9)</content>. A cold on top of chronic rhinosinusitis; no discolored drainage or facial pain to suggest another bacterial flare. The barotrauma shows why she must not fly while blocked. [S]<list><item>Continue saline irrigation and fluticasone.</item><item>Sinus CT as planned before the ten-week review.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications Reference Guide, What OTC Medications Can I Take and Still Be Safe to Fly? (2024); Ear Barotrauma (StatPearls) (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Ear and sinus review at the ten-week visit, or sooner if the ear is not clearing within two weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-17: review after ten weeks of medical therapy: symptom score, sinus CT result, and the decision about surgery. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
